{"entries":{"chapter-11-study-prevention-and-screening|Chapter 11 Study: Prevention and Screening":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Prevention acts before symptoms but can impose immediate burdens. Individualize using values, overall health, life expectancy, harms and time-to-benefit (p157).":["u3"],"copy|Many prevention trials exclude adults over 75; extrapolation to older or frail patients therefore carries uncertainty (p157).":["u4"],"copy|Consider cancer screening primarily when life expectancy exceeds 10 years, after weighing the particular test's benefits and harms (p157).":["u5"],"copy|Mammography trial meta-analysis: approximately 11 years to prevent 1 breast-cancer death per 1000 women screened (p160).":["u6"],"copy|Fecal occult blood screening: approximately 10 years to prevent 1 colorectal-cancer death per 1000 people; serious complications are also approximately 1 in 1000 (p161).":["u7"],"copy|Flexible sigmoidoscopy has a similar estimated time-to-benefit; the chapter notes that mortality timing for colonoscopy awaits ongoing trials (p161).":["u8"],"copy|Prior cervical screening is central to decisions in older women. Direct time-to-benefit data are absent for those at least 65 (p163).":["u9"],"copy|Low-dose chest CT trials show lung-cancer mortality reduction after 6 years; chest x-ray screening did not provide such benefit (p163).":["u10"],"copy|Treating asymptomatic hypertension, diabetes or dyslipidemia is also preventive: immediate treatment burdens must be balanced against delayed benefits (p157, p164).":["u11"],"copy|Intensive glycemic treatment immediately increases serious hypoglycemia risk; antihypertensive initiation appears to increase hip-fracture risk in the first 45 days (p164).":["u12"],"copy|Immunization has a more favorable near-term risk–benefit balance than many screening interventions; the chapter emphasizes influenza, pneumococcal and zoster vaccination (p165).":["u13"],"copy|Support healthy behaviors and smoking cessation; maintaining behavior change requires the patient's participation (p165, p168).":["u14"],"copy|Detecting more disease is not the same as producing net benefit during the patient's lifetime; timing of harms and benefits matters (p157, p164).":["u15"],"copy|For nursing-home residents or serious illness, prioritize near-term harms, competing needs and time-to-benefit rather than simply continuing routine preventive care (p169).":["u16"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u17"],"copy|CHAPTER 11s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 11 - Prevention and Screening; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Decision framework":["u22"],"copy|Cancer screening":["u23"],"copy|Management & prevention":["u24"],"copy|Exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u26"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (5):":["u26"],"copy|Fecal occult blood screening takes approximately 10 years to prevent 1 colorectal-cancer death per 1000 screened. Serious harm comes chiefly from downstream procedures, including bleeding or perforation during colonoscopy (p161).":["u7"],"copy|Flexible sigmoidoscopy has a similar estimated time to benefit (p161). In the later COLONPREV trial of adults aged 50–69, invitation to biennial FIT was noninferior to invitation to one-time colonoscopy for colorectal cancer mortality at ten years. That comparison does not give a time to benefit versus no screening. COLONPREV, 2025.":["u8"],"copy|Cervix. At age 65, check the actual screening history. The chapter defines adequate prior screening as three consecutive negative cytology results or two consecutive negative HPV co-tests in the preceding ten years, with the most recent within five years. A healthy older woman without adequate prior screening may still benefit. Consider whether she could undergo treatment if a lesion is found. False positives can lead to colposcopy and biopsy (p163).":["u37"],"copy|Flexible sigmoidoscopy has a similar estimated time to benefit (p161). Current practice (not the book): In the later COLONPREV trial of adults aged 50–69, invitation to biennial FIT was noninferior to invitation to one-time colonoscopy for colorectal cancer mortality at ten years. That comparison does not give a time to benefit versus no screening. COLONPREV, 2025.":["u8"],"copy|The model found biennial mammography cost-effective with life expectancy of at least 9.5 years (p161).":["u60"],"copy|Table 11-2 gives 1–5 years for hypertension treatment and 3 months–2.5 years for statins, from first detectable to near-maximal population benefit.":["u163"],"copy|The accompanying prose gives a shorter estimate of under 3 months for statins after a recent myocardial infarction, and several years for primary prevention.":["u164"],"copy|Orthostasis and falls can occur soon after starting blood pressure treatment.":["u80"],"copy|CHAPTER 11 / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|June 2023 Q85":["u18"],"copy|September 2024 Q92":["u19"],"prevention-and-screening|Prevention and Screening":["u165"],"individualizing-prevention|Individualizing prevention":["u166"],"cancer-screening|Cancer screening":["u23"],"cancer-screening-when-benefit-takes-years|Cancer screening when benefit takes years":["u33"],"other-cancer-screening-decisions|Other cancer screening decisions":["u36"],"medicines-and-immunization|Medicines and immunization":["u167"],"vaccination-by-age-disease-and-exposure|Vaccination by age, disease and exposure":["u34"],"medication-review-and-preventable-harm|Medication review and preventable harm":["u35"],"activity-nutrition-and-social-health|Activity, nutrition and social health":["u168"],"tobacco-alcohol-and-serious-illness|Tobacco, alcohol and serious illness":["u169"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u170"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u42"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u171"],"copy|Start with baseline risk and the time until a preventive intervention can help.":["u172"],"copy|Compare that interval with life expectancy, competing illness and the person's goals.":["u173"],"copy|Prevention acts before symptoms but can impose immediate burdens.":["u174"],"copy|Individualize using values, overall health, life expectancy, harms and time-to-benefit (p157).":["u175"],"copy|Pitfall.":["u176","u203"],"copy|Start with the outcome that matters to the patient.":["u46"],"copy|A test may find cancer today, yet several years may pass before screening reduces deaths.":["u47"],"copy|Estimate prognosis from function, comorbidity and age, then compare that estimate with the time to a benefit large enough to matter.":["u48"],"copy|Discuss the whole pathway, including follow-up tests and treatment.":["u49"],"copy|Figure 11-2 illustrates the range at one age: among US women aged 80, about one quarter lived five years or less and another quarter lived more than thirteen years.":["u50"],"copy|These are population estimates, not individual predictions (pp157–160, Figures 11-1 and 11-2).":["u51"],"copy|Colorectal screening and prognosis.":["u52"],"copy|Across fecal occult blood screening trials, it took about ten years to avert one colorectal cancer death per thousand people screened.":["u53"],"copy|Sigmoidoscopy trials suggested a similar interval.":["u54"],"copy|For an asymptomatic 82-year-old with prior normal screening and an estimated five years of life, switching from colonoscopy to FIT leaves the time-to-benefit problem in place.":["u55"],"copy|The chapter authors recommend stopping routine screening below a ten-year prognosis.":["u56"],"copy|Near that horizon, discuss the patient's preferences and previous screening.":["u57"],"copy|An otherwise healthy person who was never screened may have more to gain (pp161–163).":["u58"],"copy|Fecal occult blood screening takes approximately 10 years to prevent 1 colorectal-cancer death per 1000 screened.":["u177"],"copy|Serious harm comes chiefly from downstream procedures, including bleeding or perforation during colonoscopy (p161).":["u178"],"copy|Flexible sigmoidoscopy has a similar estimated time to benefit (p161).":["u179"],"copy|Current practice: FIT versus colonoscopy.":["u180"],"copy|In the later COLONPREV trial of adults aged 50–69, invitation to biennial FIT was noninferior to invitation to one-time colonoscopy for colorectal cancer mortality at ten years.":["u181"],"copy|That comparison does not give a time to benefit versus no screening.":["u182"],"copy|COLONPREV, 2025.":["u183"],"copy|What the 9.5-year figure means.":["u59"],"copy|A separate meta-analysis estimated about eleven years to avert one breast cancer death per thousand women screened.":["u61"],"copy|The authors consider biennial mammography reasonable for healthy women with more than ten years of life expectancy, even after age 75.":["u62"],"copy|None of the mammography trials summarized in the chapter included women older than 75.":["u63"],"copy|Among women aged 75 or older screened every two years, the ten-year cumulative probability of a false positive was 14%–27% and nearly doubled with annual testing.":["u64"],"copy|A false positive leads to work-up for a lesion that proves benign.":["u65"],"copy|Overdiagnosis means that a real cancer was found that would never have troubled the patient during her lifetime (pp160–161).":["u66"],"copy|What colonoscopy adds.":["u67"],"copy|An average-risk patient can choose annual stool blood testing, sigmoidoscopy every five years or colonoscopy every ten years in the chapter's comparison.":["u68"],"copy|Colonoscopy examines the colon and permits polypectomy.":["u69"],"copy|A positive FIT requires colonoscopic evaluation, so ask before ordering FIT whether the patient would pursue that evaluation.":["u70"],"copy|Bowel preparation can cause nausea or dizziness, and colonoscopy can cause bleeding, perforation and cardiopulmonary complications.":["u71"],"copy|The September 2024 question awarded credit to both the mammography and the colonoscopy answers.":["u72"],"copy|The text presents colonoscopy as one acceptable option.":["u73"],"copy|Selection at ages 76–85 depends particularly on health and prior screening (pp161–163, Table 11-1).":["u74"],"copy|Why the other two answers fell short.":["u75"],"copy|The diabetes chapter describes fasting plasma glucose or A1C testing after age 65, with the interval guided by risk.":["u76"],"copy|The geriatric assessment chapter describes a hearing question, bedside whispered voice test and office audiometry as screening approaches.":["u77"],"copy|An abnormal screen or reported difficulty can lead to diagnostic audiology (pp119 and 1561).":["u78"],"copy|A ten-year prognosis has different meanings for different treatments.":["u79"],"copy|Intensive glycemic treatment raises hypoglycemia risk now and may take eight to fifteen years to reduce vascular complications.":["u82"],"copy|A treatment aimed at distressing depressive symptoms can offer relief on a shorter time scale.":["u83"],"copy|Life expectancy matters especially in colorectal screening (pp161, 163–165, Table 11-2).":["u184"],"copy|Cervix.":["u185"],"copy|At age 65, check the actual screening history.":["u186"],"copy|The chapter defines adequate prior screening as three consecutive negative cytology results or two consecutive negative HPV co-tests in the preceding ten years, with the most recent within five years.":["u187"],"copy|A healthy older woman without adequate prior screening may still benefit.":["u188"],"copy|Consider whether she could undergo treatment if a lesion is found.":["u189"],"copy|False positives can lead to colposcopy and biopsy (p163).":["u190"],"copy|Prior cervical screening is central to decisions in older women.":["u191"],"copy|Direct time-to-benefit data are absent for those at least 65 (p163).":["u192"],"copy|Lung.":["u193"],"copy|Low-dose CT reduced lung cancer mortality in trials of heavy smokers.":["u194"],"copy|In the National Lung Screening Trial about four lung cancer deaths were averted per thousand people screened over six years.":["u195"],"copy|A positive nodule can lead to repeat imaging or invasive sampling.":["u196"],"copy|Screening offers little prospect of benefit if comorbidity precludes cancer-directed treatment.":["u197"],"copy|Chest radiography did not produce the same mortality benefit (pp163–164).":["u198"],"copy|Prostate.":["u199"],"copy|A PSA signal may trigger biopsy, and detection of a slow-growing cancer may lead to treatment with incontinence or sexual dysfunction.":["u200"],"copy|The small possible mortality benefit takes over ten years.":["u201"],"copy|The chapter authors advise against routine PSA screening of older men (p164).":["u202"],"copy|The pneumococcal sequence in the 2023 question.":["u85"],"copy|COPD increases the risk of pneumonia and exacerbations.":["u86"],"copy|The historical regimen for a previously unvaccinated 80-year-old was one PCV13 dose first, then one PPSV23 dose six to twelve months later.":["u87"],"copy|Giving both products every five years, alternating either product, or routinely adding Hib for COPD changes that regimen.":["u88"],"copy|Pain and redness at the injection site were common with either pneumococcal product, while serious reactions were rare.":["u89"],"copy|The COPD chapter starts its influenza discussion on p1255 and gives its pneumococcal advice on p1256.":["u90"],"copy|The exception in the 2022 question.":["u92"],"copy|Influenza, pneumococcal and zoster vaccines all have broad age-based indications.":["u93"],"copy|For an older adult, meningococcal vaccination depends on a specific condition or exposure, such as functional or anatomic asplenia, a complement deficiency, or a complement inhibitor.":["u94"],"copy|The serogroup-specific product depends on the indication (p165 for age-based vaccination).":["u95"],"copy|CDC, Indications for MenACWY and MenB vaccination":["u96"],"copy|Immunity and timing.":["u97"],"copy|Influenza vaccination is seasonal because winter epidemics repeatedly threaten older adults.":["u98"],"copy|The high-dose preparation discussed in the book elicited a stronger response than standard dose.":["u99"],"copy|The chapter describes Td or Tdap boosters every ten years, including a Tdap dose where prior adult pertussis vaccination is absent.":["u100"],"copy|For zoster, the recombinant glycoprotein E provides the antigen and the adjuvant strengthens the innate response.":["u101"],"copy|A two-dose recombinant series is indicated even after prior live vaccination.":["u102"],"copy|For an immunocompetent adult the usual interval is two to six months.":["u103"],"copy|Protecting against persistent postherpetic neuralgia matters as well as preventing the rash (pp165, 1256 and 1728–1729).":["u104"],"copy|An alert patient can still be harmed by a sedative.":["u105"],"copy|For the 90-year-old receiving clonazepam three times a day, review the indication, dose, duration, falls and daytime function.":["u106"],"copy|Increased sensitivity and slower elimination of long-acting benzodiazepines can contribute to delirium, impaired balance and fractures.":["u107"],"copy|Chronic use can produce physical dependence.":["u108"],"copy|Where no continuing indication justifies treatment, plan a gradual reduction with monitoring.":["u109"],"copy|Regular use alone does not diagnose a substance use disorder (p169 and p319, AGS Beers Criteria 2023, Table 2, pp2059–2060).":["u110"],"copy|The rest of the medication list still matters.":["u111"],"copy|Quetiapine warrants an indication review, and a normal cognitive examination does not tell us why it was prescribed.":["u112"],"copy|Venlafaxine and mirtazapine can contribute to adverse effects and should be reviewed against their individual benefits.":["u113"],"copy|If a regularly used opioid such as tramadol is stopped around surgery, anxiety, tremor and agitation raise concern for withdrawal.":["u114"],"copy|Review dose timing and exposures before attributing these symptoms to alcohol or benzodiazepines (p169).":["u115"],"copy|Weigh the patient and ask how weight and intake have changed.":["u116"],"copy|Use the Mini Nutritional Assessment or Malnutrition Screening Tool to characterize risk.":["u117"],"copy|When taste or appetite changes after a medication change, review the timing before adding supplements.":["u118"],"copy|Dental problems, depression, food insecurity, lonely meals and an unnecessarily restrictive diet can each reduce intake.":["u119"],"copy|A normal appearance or current BMI can conceal meaningful recent loss (pp165–166, Table 11-3).":["u120"],"copy|An older adult with arthritis or fear of falling can start with five to ten minutes of comfortable low-impact activity and increase gradually.":["u121"],"copy|Combine strength and endurance with balance and flexibility.":["u122"],"copy|Moderate activity on most days is a goal to work toward.":["u123"],"copy|Short bouts can confer similar coronary protection to a continuous session when total energy expenditure is comparable (pp166–167 and 807).":["u124"],"copy|Ask whether the patient wants more social contact and what kind.":["u125"],"copy|Someone living alone may value privacy, while someone surrounded by people may feel lonely.":["u126"],"copy|Hearing, mobility and cognitive difficulties can prevent participation in otherwise accessible groups.":["u127"],"copy|Address the identified barrier and connect the person with an activity they want.":["u128"],"copy|A positive PHQ-2 or PHQ-9 prompts clinical assessment, treatment when appropriate and follow-up (pp166–168, Table 11-3).":["u129"],"copy|Smoking cessation helps even late in life.":["u130"],"copy|Establish current use and readiness, agree on a quit attempt, offer counseling and appropriate pharmacotherapy, and arrange follow-up.":["u131"],"copy|Ask about quantity and pattern of alcohol use, injuries and interacting sedatives.":["u132"],"copy|The chapter also encourages asking directly about nonprescribed drugs.":["u133"],"copy|With obesity, assess the consequences for mobility, sleep and joint pain, while recognizing that unintentional weight loss and loss of lean mass can themselves signal risk in later life (pp168–169).":["u134"],"copy|In a nursing home or with serious illness, ask what a positive test would require of the patient, including transport, an invasive procedure or a treatment they may decline.":["u135"],"copy|Prevention can still address immediate priorities such as nutrition, manageable activity and vaccination.":["u136"],"copy|Review preventive medications whose burdens have become more immediate than their expected benefit, and involve the patient or surrogate in choosing goals (pp169–170).":["u137"],"copy|בדיקות סקר בקהילה.":["u138"],"copy|בבדיקה לאיתור סרטן המעי הגס באוכלוסייה בסיכון רגיל, משרד הבריאות מתאר בדיקת דם סמוי בצואה אחת לשנה מגיל 50 ועד 75, במסגרת הסל דרך קופות החולים.":["u139"],"copy|תוצאה חיובית מובילה להפניה לקולונוסקופיה.":["u140"],"copy|סיפור משפחתי מצריך התאמה פרטנית של המעקב.":["u141"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, בדיקות תקופתיות בגיל השלישי, סעיף דם סמוי בצואה · סרטן בגיל המבוגר, סעיף בדיקות תקופתיות":["u142"],"copy|ממוגרפיה ושמיעה.":["u143"],"copy|סל הבריאות כולל ממוגרפיה אחת לשנתיים לנשים בגילים 50–74, דרך קופת החולים ובהשתתפות עצמית לפי הזכאות לביקור במכון.":["u144"],"copy|מישוש גוש חדש מצריך בירור רפואי מיידי.":["u145"],"copy|בבירור ירידה בשמיעה מתוארת תחילה בדיקה אצל רופא המשפחה.":["u146"],"copy|לפי הממצאים אפשר להפנות לאף־אוזן־גרון ובהמשך למכון שמיעה לבדיקה אצל קלינאי תקשורת.":["u147"],"copy|בדיקות שמיעה אבחנתיות כלולות בסל.":["u148"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, בדיקות תקופתיות בגיל השלישי, סעיפי ממוגרפיה ובדיקת שמיעה, 23.09.2026 · סרטן בגיל המבוגר, סעיף בדיקות תקופתיות":["u149"],"copy|פנאומוקוק.":["u150"],"copy|משרד הבריאות מתאר כיום חיסון מצומד נגד 20 זנים.":["u151"],"copy|החיסון מומלץ מגיל 65 וכן מגיל 18 במצבי סיכון, ובהם מחלה כרונית בדרכי הנשימה.":["u152"],"copy|לרוב ניתנת מנה אחת.":["u153"],"copy|אפשר להתחסן בקופת החולים לאורך השנה ללא השתתפות עצמית.":["u154"],"copy|השילוב PCV13 ואחריו PPSV23 שייך ללוח המתואר בספר ולשאלת יוני 2023.":["u155"],"copy|משרד הב